.TH NS2MMD 1
.SH NAME
ns2mmd \- the machines a name space reaches, as a diagram
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ns2mmd
[
.B -a
] [
.B -n
.I name
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ns2mmd
reads the lines of a name space, as
.IR ns (1)
writes them, from its standard input, and writes a Mermaid flowchart
of the machines they reach
.RI ( mermaid (2)):
one node for this machine, named by
.B /dev/sysname
or
.IR name ;
one for each other machine a mount reaches; an edge from it for each
mount, labelled with its service and mount point, and
.B encrypted
for one over
.IR ssl (3).
.PP
Its input is a stream: a blank line, or the end, ends each name space,
and each is a chart, ended by a line holding only a form feed, unless
it is the one written last.
Piped into
.B wm/page -s
.RI ( page (1)),
the diagram follows the name space as it changes:
.IP
.EX
{while {} {ns; echo; sleep 1}} | ns2mmd | wm/page -s
.EE
.PP
A mount's source is found by reading files: a dial string
.RB ( tcp!pi5!video ,
as a mount made without encryption shows it) as it is; an encrypted
conversation's
.RB ( #D/0/data )
through its
.B lower
file to its connection; a connection's
.RB ( /net/tcp/3/data )
through its
.BR remote .
An address is named by ndb
.RB ( /lib/ndb/local ,
.BR inferno ,
.BR common ):
a machine by
.BR sys= ,
a port by its
.B tcp=
service.
These files are the machine's that
.I ns2mmd
runs on: the name space of another machine (from
.IR cpu (1))
names connections it cannot read.
.PP
Mounts of local servers (pipes, file servers started here) are left
out; with
.BR -a ,
each is a node of its own, named by its source.
.SH EXAMPLE
On a Mac with a Pi's camera and an Orin's recognition mounted:
.IP
.EX
; ns | ns2mmd
flowchart LR
    me["mac"]
    h0["pi5<br/>192.168.1.100"]
    h1["orin<br/>10.147.17.120"]
    h0 -->|"video · /mnt/video<br/>encrypted"| me
    h1 -->|"vision · /mnt/vision<br/>encrypted"| me
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ns2mmd.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ns (1),
.IR page (1),
.IR mermaid (2),
.IR ssl (3)
